{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ETWAD3LpcwoZ1kxpcYFVSw","type":"tool_result","content":"./k8s-docs-cluster-addons-2026-08-24.md\n./k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23.md\n./k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md\n./k8s-docs-network-policies-2026-08-23.md\n=====CNI MENTIONS=====\n./k8s-docs-cluster-addons-2026-08-24.md:22:[Entries for ACI, Antrea, Canal, CNI-Genie, Contiv, Contrail, Gateway API, Knitter, kube-router, Multus, OVN-Kubernetes, Nodus, NSX-T, Nuage, Romana, Spiderpool, Terway and Weave Net trimmed as out of scope for Chapter 3 - see the source URL for the full list.]\n./k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23.md:23:- **kube-proxy (optional)** — kube-proxy is a network proxy that runs on each node in your cluster, implementing part of the Kubernetes Service concept. kube-proxy maintains network rules on nodes. These network rules allow network communication to your Pods from network sessions inside or outside of your cluster. If you use a network plugin that implements packet forwarding for Services by itself, and providing equivalent behavior to kube-proxy, then you do not need to run kube-proxy on the nodes in your cluster.\n./k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md:15:- Binary plugins — Kubernetes executes external binaries; used by the kubelet (CSI storage plugins, CNI network plugins) and by kubectl (plugins).\n./k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md:23:6. Infrastructure extensions — Device Plugins (custom hardware); Storage plugins (CSI, the Container Storage Interface, to add new storage types); Network plugins (CNI, the Container Network Interface, to implement pod networking); Container runtime (CRI, the Container Runtime Interface, to support alternative container runtimes); kubelet image credential provider plugins.\n./k8s-docs-network-policies-2026-08-23.md:15:Network policies are implemented by the network plugin. To use network policies, you must be using a networking solution which supports NetworkPolicy. Creating a NetworkPolicy resource without a controller that implements it will have no effect.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"12c0cc88-5316-4f2f-97c5-fd9663367ffb","uuid":"a2220ff5-ae3e-4efb-b46c-efaebb089040","timestamp":"2026-08-25T00:37:15.503Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"./k8s-docs-cluster-addons-2026-08-24.md\n./k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23.md\n./k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md\n./k8s-docs-network-policies-2026-08-23.md\n=====CNI MENTIONS=====\n./k8s-docs-cluster-addons-2026-08-24.md:22:[Entries for ACI, Antrea, Canal, CNI-Genie, Contiv, Contrail, Gateway API, Knitter, kube-router, Multus, OVN-Kubernetes, Nodus, NSX-T, Nuage, Romana, Spiderpool, Terway and Weave Net trimmed as out of scope for Chapter 3 - see the source URL for the full list.]\n./k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23.md:23:- **kube-proxy (optional)** — kube-proxy is a network proxy that runs on each node in your cluster, implementing part of the Kubernetes Service concept. kube-proxy maintains network rules on nodes. These network rules allow network communication to your Pods from network sessions inside or outside of your cluster. If you use a network plugin that implements packet forwarding for Services by itself, and providing equivalent behavior to kube-proxy, then you do not need to run kube-proxy on the nodes in your cluster.\n./k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md:15:- Binary plugins — Kubernetes executes external binaries; used by the kubelet (CSI storage plugins, CNI network plugins) and by kubectl (plugins).\n./k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md:23:6. Infrastructure extensions — Device Plugins (custom hardware); Storage plugins (CSI, the Container Storage Interface, to add new storage types); Network plugins (CNI, the Container Network Interface, to implement pod networking); Container runtime (CRI, the Container Runtime Interface, to support alternative container runtimes); kubelet image credential provider plugins.\n./k8s-docs-network-policies-2026-08-23.md:15:Network policies are implemented by the network plugin. To use network policies, you must be using a networking solution which supports NetworkPolicy. Creating a NetworkPolicy resource without a controller that implements it will have no effect.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
